---
import { Picture } from "astro:assets";
import { heroPhoto } from "../data/gallery";
import { directionsUrl, primaryPhone } from "../data/contact";
import { nbsp } from "../utils/typography";

/**
 * The entrance to the home page: the heading and what to do, beside a portrait of the
 * holding.
 *
 * ## The September 2026 rebuild, and the four site rules it suspends
 *
 * The owners supplied a rendered comparison of this band and asked for it to be reproduced
 * exactly. Three things in it are departures from rules written down in `CLAUDE.md`, and each
 * one is here because they asked for it after the rule was put to them - not because the rule
 * was forgotten. They are listed here so that nobody "fixes" them back:
 *
 * 1. **Two hand-drawn sprigs and a handset, and they are icons.** The design is austere and the
 *    rule is "no icons, no emoji", with one named exemption for the Facebook block and an
 *    explicit instruction not to spread it to a fourth file. This is that fourth file. They are
 *    inline SVG rather than `astro-icon`: nothing is added to the dependency list, nothing is
 *    fetched, and the exemption stays where it can be seen. **Do not lift them into
 *    `src/icons/` or reuse them elsewhere** - the moment a second component draws a sprig, the
 *    site has an icon set and the rule is gone rather than suspended.
 * 2. **`text-wrap: balance` is gone from the heading.** The reference breaks the heading
 *    long-then-short ("balkonowych, rabatowych" / "i chryzantem"), which is what a line box
 *    does on its own; `balance` exists to prevent exactly that and had to come off. `--text-h1`
 *    did **not** have to move with it: measured in the widened column, 60px breaks this heading
 *    exactly where the reference does, so the approved display size survives the rebuild.
 * 3. **The heading is in two voices without being in two typefaces.** "Producent kwiatów" is
 *    green and carries the sprig, but it is the same upright Fraunces as the ink half. The
 *    italic was tried twice here - on "Producent" alone and on the whole phrase - and rejected
 *    both times, so the masthead is again the only italic on the site.
 *
 * **Two marks from the reference are gone and are not to be restored on the strength of it.**
 * It drew a 1px rule trailing off the overline and another trailing off the green half of the
 * heading; the owners had both removed, the overline's first and the heading's after it. The
 * sprig is what closes that line now. The rule in the divider above the facts is a different
 * thing and stays - it separates two blocks rather than decorating a line of type.
 *
 * ## The two columns
 *
 * `flex-wrap` rather than the `auto-fit` grid every other two-column block here uses, and the
 * reason is arithmetic rather than taste. `repeat(auto-fit, minmax(…, 1fr))` can only make
 * columns of **equal** width, and equal columns give the text 572px at 1400px - narrow enough
 * that the heading breaks into four lines with three short ones. The reference gives the text
 * roughly three fifths of the band. `flex-wrap` buys that asymmetry and still stacks on a
 * phone with **no media query**, so the project's zero-breakpoint rule survives intact.
 * Verified at 360px: one column, no horizontal scroll.
 *
 * ## The photograph, and why it is the wrong one
 *
 * **This frame is a placeholder for a photograph that does not exist yet, and it is the most
 * visible gap left on the site.** The figure is 4:5, and the holding has no free portrait
 * frame to put in it:
 *
 * - the 23 frames in `src/assets/gallery/` are all portrait and all sharp, but every one of
 *   them is a planting in the `compositions` collection, and that strip runs three sections
 *   below this one - the hero would reappear as a slide on the same page;
 * - `chrysanthemums/offer-0*` are portrait but only 736px wide, which is under one device
 *   pixel of this box on any modern screen;
 * - `pansies/offer-0*` are portrait and large, but they show March crates of pansies, and a
 *   year-round hero for a holding whose main season is April to June cannot be one product
 *   photographed in the wrong month.
 *
 * So `heroPhoto` stays, and it is cropped: it is 1448x1086 (4:3), a generated image rather
 * than a photograph from the session, and a 4:5 box shows about 60% of its width. The crop is
 * done once, by `object-fit` in CSS, and aimed by `object-position` - `width`/`height` below
 * carry the file's real ratio so that Astro only scales.
 *
 * **What to do when a portrait frame arrives:** swap `heroPhoto` in `src/data/gallery.ts`,
 * put the file's real dimensions in `width`/`height` here, and delete this section of the
 * comment. Nothing else changes. The request is on the owners' list in
 * `docs/inwentaryzacja.md`.
 *
 * `loading="eager"` and `fetchpriority="high"` - this is the home page's LCP and there is no
 * point pretending otherwise.
 */
---

<section class="intro">
  <div class="intro__text">
    <p class="eyebrow intro__place">Cholewianka pod Kazimierzem Dolnym</p>
    <h1 class="intro__title">
      {
        /* The `{" "}` is load-bearing and not a stray. `.intro__lead` is a block, so the two
          halves sit on their own lines whatever happens between them - but without an explicit
          space the compiler collapses the newline away and the `h1` reads
          "Producent kwiatówbalkonowych" to anything that takes its text rather than its
          layout: a screen reader, a copy-paste, and a search engine indexing a site that
          ranks on exactly these words. */
      }
      <span class="intro__lead"
        >Producent <span class="intro__lead-last"
          >kwiatów<svg
            class="intro__sprig intro__sprig--lead"
            viewBox="0 0 24 16"
            aria-hidden="true"
            focusable="false"
          >
            <path d="M1 15C7 14 13.2 10.2 21 1.6" />
            <path d="M21 1.6c-4 .6-6.6 2.6-7.2 6.2 4-.4 6.6-2.4 7.2-6.2Z" />
            <path d="M12.4 10.4c-3-2-4.2-4.9-3.4-8.2 3 1.9 4.2 4.8 3.4 8.2Z" />
          </svg></span
        ></span
      >{" "}
      {nbsp("balkonowych, rabatowych i chryzantem")}
    </h1>
    {
      /* **Two sentences, not three.** The handoff writes a third here - "Wszystko, co
        sprzedajemy, pochodzi z naszej własnej uprawy." - and it shipped until the owners had it
        taken out as redundant. `docs/design/README.md` § "Intro" and the v3 prototype both
        still carry it, so they now disagree with this file on purpose; do not restore it from
        them. The claim itself is not lost: the "Uprawa / Własna" fact below still makes it, as
        do the page's meta description, /o-nas/ and the FAQ. */
    }
    <p class="intro__body">
      Zachęcamy do zapoznania się z naszą ofertą. Zamówienia można składać telefonicznie lub na
      terenie naszego gospodarstwa w Cholewiance pod Kazimierzem Dolnym.
    </p>
    <div class="intro__actions">
      <a class="cta intro__call" href={primaryPhone.href}>
        Zadzwoń: {primaryPhone.display}
        <svg class="intro__handset" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
          <path
            d="M6.4 3h3l1.5 4-2 1.5a12 12 0 0 0 5.6 5.6l1.5-2 4 1.5v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2A17 17 0 0 1 4.4 5.2 2 2 0 0 1 6.4 3Z"
          />
        </svg>
      </a>
      <a class="cta-line" href="/kwiaty-balkonowe/">Zobacz ofertę</a>
      <a class="cta-line" href={directionsUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wyznacz trasę</a>
    </div>
    {
      /* The rule that used to sit on `.intro__facts` as a `border-top`, now drawn as its own
        element so the sprig can stand in the middle of it. Decoration with nothing to announce,
        so it is hidden from the accessibility tree entirely. */
    }
    <div class="intro__divider" aria-hidden="true">
      <svg class="intro__sprig intro__sprig--divider" viewBox="0 0 24 16" focusable="false">
        <path d="M1 15C7 14 13.2 10.2 21 1.6" />
        <path d="M21 1.6c-4 .6-6.6 2.6-7.2 6.2 4-.4 6.6-2.4 7.2-6.2Z" />
        <path d="M12.4 10.4c-3-2-4.2-4.9-3.4-8.2 3 1.9 4.2 4.8 3.4 8.2Z" />
      </svg>
    </div>
    {
      /* Three facts, each one a restatement of a sentence this site already says elsewhere -
        nothing here is new information, which is the point: it is the paragraph above made
        scannable for someone who will not read it. */
    }
    <dl class="intro__facts">
      <div>
        <dt>Uprawa</dt>
        <dd>Własna</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Sprzedaż</dt>
        <dd>Na miejscu</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Zamówienia</dt>
        <dd>Telefonicznie</dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
  </div>

  <div class="intro__figure">
    <Picture
      class="intro__photo"
      src={heroPhoto.src}
      formats={["avif", "webp"]}
      fallbackFormat="jpg"
      widths={[720, 1448]}
      sizes="(min-width: 1200px) 620px, (min-width: 700px) 42vw, 92vw"
      width={1448}
      height={1086}
      alt={heroPhoto.alt}
      loading="eager"
      fetchpriority="high"
    />
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  /* **--paper, and it is the lightest ground on the site for a reason that is about the section
     below rather than about this one.** The hero stood on --paper-blush until September 2026,
     when the news block moved up directly under it: blush against the news block's --paper-clay
     is 1.003:1, so the two bands were one field of paper cut by a hairline, and the hairline
     read as a mistake. --paper makes that step 1.079:1 - the strongest this palette allows
     between two light grounds, because --ink-grey needs a ground no darker than L 0.8353.
     It is the ceiling, not a preference, and the direction is right: the page is lightest at
     the top and darkens down. See tokens.css at the grounds, where blush's value is kept. */
  /* Flex rather than the site's usual `auto-fit` grid - the header comment says why, and the
     short version is that an `auto-fit` grid cannot make one column wider than the other. */
  .intro {
    background: var(--paper);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
    padding: var(--pad-section) var(--edge);
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--gap-columns);
    align-items: center;
  }

  /* **560px, and the basis is what decides when the band splits in two.** At 420px the two
     columns fitted side by side from about 1000px up, which handed the text a 479px column
     while `--text-h1` was already near its 60px maximum - "Producent kwiatów" alone then took
     two lines and the heading ran to six. The basis is the width below which two columns are a
     worse answer than one, so it belongs at the width the heading actually needs: 560px holds
     the green phrase on one line at full display size. Below that the band stacks and the text
     gets the whole shell, which reads better than a squeezed column beside a photograph. */
  .intro__text {
    flex: 1 1 560px;
    display: grid;
    gap: 1.15rem;
    justify-items: start;
  }

  /* The village is named here *and* in the paragraph below, on purpose: an uppercase label is
     a poor sole hiding place for the most important fact on the site of a holding people drive
     to, and a label weighs less in a search engine than a sentence in prose.

     **No trailing rule.** The reference drew one running off the end of this label and it
     shipped for one release; the owners had it taken out, and the heading's own rule followed
     it two releases later. Neither comes back without them asking. */
  .intro__place {
    font-size: 0.68rem;
  }

  .intro__title {
    font-size: var(--text-h1);
    letter-spacing: -0.015em;
    line-height: 1.04;
    width: 100%;
    /* No `text-wrap: balance` here, and its absence is deliberate - see point 3 of the header
       comment. The reference breaks this heading long-then-short, which is what a line box does
       unaided; `balance` exists to even lines out and would undo it. */
  }

  /* --green rather than --sage: this runs at display size, but it is a fragment of a heading
     and not the heading, so it is held to the text green's 4.95:1 rather than to the large-type
     exception.

     **Upright, and the italic is not coming back.** It sat on "Producent" alone, then on
     "Producent kwiatów", and the owners rejected both - the slant on a phrase this long reads
     as a different voice rather than as an accent. What carries the level is the colour and the
     sprig, which is enough. The masthead is once again the only italic on the site, which is
     what `fonts.css` and `Header.astro` have both claimed all along.

     **A 1px rule ran from the sprig to the edge of the column for three releases and the owners
     had it removed.** `display: block` is what is left of it: the rule needed a flex row to
     grow into, and without it this only has to be a block so the ink half starts on its own
     line. Do not put the rule back to "balance" the line - the sprig is deliberately the last
     thing on it. */
  .intro__lead {
    display: block;
    color: var(--green);
  }

  /* Both sprigs. Stroke-drawn at a hair over 1px, which is the weight every other line on this
     page is drawn at. */
  .intro__sprig {
    fill: none;
    stroke: currentColor;
    stroke-width: 1.1;
    stroke-linecap: round;
    stroke-linejoin: round;
  }

  .intro__body {
    max-width: 52ch;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }

  .intro__actions {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.7rem 1.4rem;
    align-items: center;
  }

  /* The handset rides inside the existing `.cta`, which is already an inline-flex row, so this
     only has to space it and size it. `--paper` comes from `.cta`'s own colour by inheritance. */
  .intro__call {
    gap: 0.7rem;
  }

  .intro__handset {
    flex: none;
    width: 1.35em;
    height: 1.35em;
    fill: none;
    stroke: currentColor;
    stroke-width: 1.7;
    stroke-linecap: round;
    stroke-linejoin: round;
  }

  .intro__divider {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 1.1rem;
    width: 100%;
    margin-top: 0.35rem;
    color: var(--green);
  }

  .intro__divider::before,
  .intro__divider::after {
    content: "";
    flex: 1;
    height: 1px;
    background: var(--rule);
  }

  /* The divider draws this block's top line now, so there is no `border-top` here any more -
     one horizontal line, not two stacked a rem apart. */
  /* 0.56em/0.22em rather than something rounder, and the six pixels are not cosmetic. At 390px -
     the commonest phone width - the phrase plus a larger sprig needed 339px against 335px
     available, so it broke into two lines for want of four pixels. This size brings it to about
     332px and buys back a whole line of the heading. Below roughly 375px it wraps anyway,
     cleanly, at the space between the two words, because `.intro__lead-last` keeps the leaf
     attached to "kwiatów". */
  .intro__sprig--lead {
    display: inline-block;
    vertical-align: middle;
    width: 0.56em;
    height: 0.37em;
    margin-left: 0.22em;
  }

  /* The sprig is its own inline box, so a line break can fall between "kwiatów" and it - and at
     390px one did, leaving the phrase on one line and the leaf alone on the next. Tying it to
     the last word is the same job `nbsp()` does for the single-letter words in the ink half. */
  .intro__lead-last {
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  .intro__sprig--divider {
    flex: none;
    width: 1.4rem;
    height: 0.95rem;
  }

  /* **160px, and the floor is chosen for the phone rather than for the desktop.** At 130px this
     grid went 2 + 1 on a phone, which leaves "Zamówienia" alone on a second row next to an
     empty half - it reads as something that did not fit rather than as a decision. 160px is the
     smallest floor that makes two columns impossible in a 335px text column, so the three facts
     stack into one tidy list there, go two across on a tablet and three across on a desktop.
     All three states are correct, which is the whole point of picking the floor instead of
     writing a breakpoint. */
  .intro__facts {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, 160px), 1fr));
    gap: 0.9rem 1.75rem;
    width: 100%;
    /* Load-bearing, not tidiness - it is what clips the separators off the first item in
       **every** row. See the rule below. */
    overflow: hidden;
  }

  .intro__facts > div {
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.1rem;
  }

  .intro__facts dt {
    font-size: 0.62rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.18em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--ink-grey);
  }

  .intro__facts dd {
    font-family: var(--serif);
    font-size: 1.2rem;
    margin: 0;
  }

  /* The separators the reference draws between the three facts, and getting them to survive
     wrapping is the whole difficulty. `div + div` is wrong and was tried: it selects on DOM
     order, not on position, so when this `auto-fit` grid drops to two columns the third fact
     starts a row **carrying a separator at the left margin with nothing to its left**. Measured
     at 500px, where the grid goes 2 + 1.

     So every item draws its own line **into the gap on its left**, and the leftmost one in each
     row has it clipped away: the line is absolutely positioned at half the column gap to the
     left of the item, which for the first item of a row is outside the grid's content box, and
     `overflow: hidden` on the grid cuts it off there. That happens on every row, however many
     rows the `auto-fit` arithmetic decides on - no breakpoint, no counting, nothing to keep in
     sync with the `minmax()` floor above.

     Absolutely positioned rather than a `border-left` with padding, and that is the second
     thing this had to get right: padding would indent the first fact by the gap width and
     break its alignment with the paragraph and the button above it. This way the separators
     cost the layout nothing at all. */
  .intro__facts > div {
    position: relative;
  }

  .intro__facts > div::before {
    content: "";
    position: absolute;
    left: -0.875rem; /* half of the 1.75rem column gap */
    top: 0;
    bottom: 0;
    width: 1px;
    background: var(--rule);
  }

  /* The frame. `<Picture>` renders `<picture><img>`, so the ratio sits on this wrapper and the
     image fills it - putting `aspect-ratio` on the `img` alone leaves the wrapper at the
     intrinsic height and opens a gap under the photograph.

     `max-width` is what makes the band asymmetric: the text takes the room the photograph
     gives up. Without it `flex: 1 1 320px` would grow the figure to match the text again. */
  /* `--hero-lift` is the site's second shadow and the only one outside the news block; the
     boundary and the reason are written at the token in `tokens.css`. The 1px border stays with
     it rather than being replaced by it: the border is what draws the frame's edge against the
     paper, and the shadow only puts the frame slightly above it. Dropping the border and
     leaving the shadow makes the photograph float with no edge, which is a different design
     from this one. */
  .intro__figure {
    flex: 1 1 320px;
    max-width: 480px;
    aspect-ratio: 4 / 5;
    border: 1px solid var(--rule);
    box-shadow: var(--hero-lift);
  }

  .intro__figure > :global(picture) {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
  }

  /* The only crop of this photograph, so it is aimed rather than left in the middle. The file
     is 4:3 and the box is 4:5, so about 40% of its width is outside the frame; 42% pulls the
     hanging baskets up into it instead of leaving the glasshouse roof there. Beats the
     `height: auto` that global.css puts on every img. */
  .intro__photo {
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    object-fit: cover;
    object-position: center 42%;
  }
</style>
